This PR implements a new event-replay import mode named `preorg` which is about an order of magnitude faster than previous modes.

It performs a fast "re-org" pass over a tsv file, generating a new tsv that only includes canonical data. Events from this pre-processed tsv are then inserted more-directly into postgres, bypassing the various sql queries required for regular re-org handling, and also bypassing the need for table indexes to be enabled (and thus slowing down the process) while inserting.

This new mode can also take advantage of batched INSERTs which are generally 2-5x faster than INSERTing one at a time.

BTW h/t to @aulneau who inspired the concept behind this idea a while back. Thank you!

So far this PR implements the tsv canonical data scan code, "preorg'd" tsv generation, batched SQL INSERTs, and ability to perform classic event import for last N (default 256) blocks.

----

Additionally, a new env var `PG_IDENT_INDEX_TYPE` has been introduced which controls the sql index method used on identifier columns (e.g. `tx_id`, `block_hash`, etc). Previously these columns used in index method `hash`, now, if the env var is not specified, the default method `btree` is used. The `hash` method is faster for reads for slower for writes, while `btree` is faster for writes and slower for reads. Running event-import with `PG_IDENT_INDEX_TYPE=hash` results in the full process taking around 15 minutes. When using the default (`btree`), the full process takes ~7 minutes. Ephemeral / non-production deployments should take advantage of `btree` for faster event-replays, while production deployments should use `hash` for scalability. 

----

This mode takes around 7-8 minutes to run an event import using a full archival 4.7 GB tsv file. 

